  "problem": "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}-12\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+6\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}+12=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $3{y}_{1}^{2}-7{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$",
